        <div id="online-vs-legacy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Online Brands vs Legacy Brands: Who Makes Your Mattress
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mattress brands fall into three groups, and knowing which group a brand belongs to tells you a lot about its prices, its policies and its range.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Online-First Brands</h3>

          <p>
            Nectar, Casper, Purple and Tuft & Needle started by selling mattresses in a box directly to buyers. They built the long trial period into the business, because it replaced the showroom test. Their ranges are short, their prices sit in the middle to upper tier, and their trial and warranty terms are usually the most generous.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Legacy Brands</h3>

          <p>
            Sealy and Serta made mattresses for decades before boxes existed, and most of their beds are still sold through stores. Their boxed lines, Dreamlife and Sleeptrue, are separate products built for shipping. You get decades of coil engineering at an online price, but not the same bed you would test in a showroom.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Budget Direct Brands</h3>

          <p>
            Zinus, Linenspa and Novilla sell mostly through Amazon at the lowest prices in the category. They make good guest beds and first beds, with lower-density foams and shorter or unstated trials. Several of their mattresses appear in our main <a href="/best-mattress/">top rated mattresses</a> guide.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Shared Ownership</h3>

          <p>
            Brands change hands. Amazon listings for Tuft & Needle, for example, name Serta Simmons Bedding as the manufacturer. Ownership rarely changes the bed you get, but it can change who handles a warranty claim, so keep the listing, the receipt and the law tag.
          </p>
        </div>

