        <div id="by-sleeper" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which Mattress Brand Is Best for Your Sleep Style
          </h2>

          <p>
            Brand matters less than model, but each brand's signature construction does suit some sleepers better than others.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Side Sleepers</h3>

          <p>
            Side sleepers need give at the shoulder and hip. Casper's zoned foam and Nectar's memory foam both do this well. Our <a href="/best-mattress-for-side-sleepers/">best mattress for side sleepers</a> guide goes model by model.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Back Sleepers and Back Discomfort</h3>

          <p>
            Most back sleepers prefer medium firm, which Nectar, Sealy and Casper all offer. Many people with lower back discomfort prefer a medium-firm bed with zoned support. A mattress is not a treatment, so talk to your doctor or physical therapist about persistent pain; our <a href="/best-mattress-for-back-pain/">best mattress for back pain</a> guide covers the options.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hot Sleepers</h3>

          <p>
            Purple's grid is the most different approach to heat. Hybrids from Casper, Tuft & Needle and Serta also breathe better than all-foam beds.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Heavier Sleepers and Couples</h3>

          <p>
            Heavier sleepers and couples benefit from thicker hybrids with strong edges, such as Sealy's Dreamlife or Serta's zoned Sleeptrue. See our guide to the <a href="/best-mattress-for-heavy-people/">best mattresses for heavy people</a>.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Older Sleepers</h3>

          <p>
            Edge support and bed height matter most for getting in and out. Sealy's edge coils help; our <a href="/best-mattress-for-seniors/">best mattress for seniors</a> guide covers the rest.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="price-tiers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Mattress Brand Price Tiers: Budget, Mid-Range and Premium
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mattress brands sort into price tiers more cleanly than almost any other product, and the tier tells you roughly what you are paying for. Prices move constantly with sales, so compare tiers rather than sticker prices.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Budget: Zinus, Linenspa, Novilla</h3>

          <p>
            Budget brands sell 10 and 12 inch foam and simple hybrid beds for a fraction of the premium price. They cut cost with lower-density foam, thinner comfort layers, continuous coils instead of pocketed ones, and shorter or unstated trials. For a guest room or a child's bed, that is a sensible trade. For a primary bed you plan to keep for a decade, expect to replace a budget mattress sooner.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Mid-Range: Tuft & Needle, Serta Sleeptrue, Sealy Dreamlife, Nectar</h3>

          <p>
            Mid-range brands add denser foams, pocketed or zoned coils, reinforced edges and stated trials of 100 nights or more. This is where most buyers get the best value, and where legacy brands compete hardest with the online brands. Nectar sits at the low end of this tier with the longest trial on the list, which is why it ranks first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Premium: Casper, Purple</h3>

          <p>
            Premium brands charge for signature construction, such as Casper's zoning or Purple's grid, plus polished support and a recognizable name. The extra money buys a specific feel, not automatically a longer life. Wait for a sale if you can; our <a href="/mattress-prices-and-deals/">mattress prices and deals</a> guide explains when brands discount.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where the Money Actually Goes</h3>

          <p>
            Within a brand's range, price rises with thickness, coil count and comfort layer depth. Across brands, a large share of the price difference is marketing and policy. A mid-range hybrid with a 100-night trial is often a better buy than a premium foam bed without one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Setting Up a Brand-Name Mattress in a Box
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every brand on this list ships compressed and rolled, and the setup steps they publish are almost identical. Following them protects the trial and the warranty.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Expansion Time</h3>

          <p>
            Serta and Linenspa state up to 72 hours for full expansion, and Novilla says about the same. Most beds are usable within a few hours, but do not judge the firmness on the first night.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Off-Gassing</h3>

          <p>
            New foam has a smell that fades with fresh air. Serta says one to two weeks; owners of budget foam beds such as Zinus commonly report several days. Open the windows and leave the sheets off for the first day.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Base Each Brand Expects</h3>

          <p>
            Most brands require a solid platform, slats spaced closely, or a compatible adjustable base, and an unsupportive base can void the warranty. Check the brand's spacing requirement before you reuse an old frame or box spring, and add a <a href="/best-mattress-topper/">mattress topper</a> later only if you want to change the feel.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="model-matters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Brand vs Model: Why the Model Matters More
          </h2>

          <p>
            A brand name narrows the field, but the model decides what you sleep on. Most brands now sell several firmness levels and constructions under the same name, and the difference between two models from one brand can be bigger than the difference between two brands.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Same Name, Different Bed</h3>

          <p>
            Ashley sells medium firm, plush and ultra plush beds under the Chime name. Zinus sells original, Essential and Cooling Green Tea mattresses that look almost identical in photos. Tuft & Needle has current and Legacy versions of the Original. Always read the full listing title for firmness, thickness and version.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Showroom Lines vs Box Lines</h3>

          <p>
            Sealy Posturepedic and Serta's store lines are not the same beds as Dreamlife and Sleeptrue. If a friend recommends a Sealy, ask which one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Compare by Spec, Then by Brand</h3>

          <p>
            Choose firmness and construction first, using our main <a href="/best-mattress/">top rated mattresses</a> guide, then use brand as the tie-breaker for trial, warranty and support. That order prevents most bad purchases.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-amazon" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Buying Brand-Name Mattresses on Amazon: Sellers and Returns
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every brand on this list sells on Amazon, and most run official storefronts there. A few checks make the purchase safer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check the Seller</h3>

          <p>
            Look for sold by the brand or by Amazon. A third-party seller may not honour the brand's trial or warranty, and some sell older versions under current names.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Read the Return Terms on the Listing</h3>

          <p>
            A stated brand trial usually applies to authorized sellers, but the return process can involve both the brand and Amazon. Read the listing's return terms before you order, especially for heavy beds like the Sealy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Know the Certifications</h3>

          <p>
            Most brands here list CertiPUR-US certified foam, which is tested for certain emissions and substances. Several list their mattresses as fiberglass free, which matters if you plan to remove the cover. Treat these as the brand's statements, and check the current listing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Watch for Older Versions</h3>

          <p>
            Brands update their beds every few years and older stock keeps selling. Tuft & Needle labels some earlier models Legacy, Casper has a 2019 Original Hybrid still listed, and Nectar has several Classic listings, some marked unavailable. Check the model year or version in the title and the product details, and compare thickness and layers with the brand's current page before you order. An older version is fine at the right price, but it should be a choice, not a surprise.
          </p>
        </div>

